Our Go metrics service has a counter that silently loses increments under load. Here is the current implementation:

```go
// store/store.go
package store

// Counter is a string-keyed counter shared across goroutines.
type Counter struct {
	values map[string]int
}

func New() *Counter {
	return &Counter{values: make(map[string]int)}
}

func (c *Counter) Incr(key string) {
	c.values[key]++
}

func (c *Counter) Get(key string) int {
	return c.values[key]
}

func (c *Counter) Keys() []string {
	out := make([]string, 0, len(c.values))
	for k := range c.values {
		out = append(out, k)
	}
	return out
}
```

Fix the data race while keeping the exported API (New, Incr, Get, Keys) unchanged, and make Keys() return a sorted snapshot so callers can iterate deterministically.

Reply with:
1. The complete fixed store.go in a single go code block.
2. A complete store_test.go in a single go code block with table-driven tests covering: single-goroutine increments, concurrent increments across 64 goroutines and 1000 keys (verify the exact total using sync.WaitGroup), and Keys() ordering.
3. A short "Root cause" section of at most 100 words explaining what was wrong and why your fix is safe.
